# {{ project_name }} — Engineering Standards

An **engineering standards hierarchy**. Each layer constrains the layers below it. Read it top
down, from business intent to delivery mechanics.

## Status

Every document carries a `status` in its frontmatter:

| status | Meaning | May an agent follow it? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `not-started` | No rules yet. | **No.** Never infer a rule from it — ask a human. |
| `proposed` | Baseline defaults, not yet adopted by this project. | Only as a suggestion; flag that it is unratified. |
| `in-progress` | Being decided. Rules may still change. | Provisionally, and say so. |
| `ratified` | Binding. Changing it requires an ADR. | **Yes.** |

Only a human moves a document to `ratified`. The `ratify-standard` skill runs that session.

## The hierarchy

```text
                        BUSINESS  (01)
                            │
                          DOMAIN  (02)
                            │
                  ARCHITECTURE  (03, 04)
                            │
       PLATFORM STANDARDS  (05–09)   protocols · security · observability · reliability · errors
                            │
       ENGINEERING RULES   (10–13)   patterns · style · testing · technology
                            │
              DELIVERY  (14, 15)     git · CI/CD
                            │
           AI OPERATIONS  (16)
                            │
                 SPECS  (17)  ──────►  a feature spec cuts vertically through every layer

        PACKS (docs/packs/)  ──────►  optional layers; depend on the core, never the reverse
```

A spec is an **input**, never an override: where a feature appears to need an exception to a
standard, that exception is an ADR.

## Layers

| # | Layer | Decides |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 01 | `01-business/` | Scope, non-goals, the priority order when goals conflict, compliance posture |
| 02 | `02-domain/` | Ubiquitous language, identifiers, time, lifecycles and invariants |
| 03 | `03-architecture/` | System shape, boundaries, dependency direction |
| 04 | `04-adr/` | Why each significant decision was made |
| 05 | `05-protocols/` | Which published standards (RFCs, ISO, specs) are adopted |
| 06 | `06-security/` | Application security, authn/authz, sensitive data, secrets |
| 07 | `07-observability/` | Logging, metrics, tracing, health |
| 08 | `08-reliability/` | Timeouts, retries, concurrency, idempotency |
| 09 | `09-errors/` | Error taxonomy and handling |
| 10 | `10-patterns/` | Approved patterns and named anti-patterns |
| 11 | `11-style/` | Naming and code style principles |
| 12 | `12-testing/` | Test levels, coverage, what must be tested |
| 13 | `13-technology/` | The stack: one document per technology decision |
| 14 | `14-git/` | Branching, commits, pull requests, review |
| 15 | `15-delivery/` | CI gates, deployments, rollback, production readiness |
| 16 | `16-ai/` | Agent autonomy, operating procedure, prohibitions, model routing |
| 17 | `17-specs/` | Spec lifecycle, templates, work tracking |

